> But one central point was portability of the data, thus exchangability
> of save files. You may want to re-think my critique in this light.

That's a good point, but a far more difficult problem to solve.

One obvious solution is to only allow exchange files with "trusted"
servers (on which "white hats" control the game binaries and
filesystem, and the public connects over the network to play).

Another harder and less-obvious solution would be to allow all comers,
but require that the files in question carry a full history which
would allow validation. That means recording the PRNG seed, every
input, every move so that the receiver could "play back" the whole
game and verify they'd arrived at the same result.

This is obviously a huge amount of work, but is cool in that it allows
distributed play and source hacks so long as they modify only the
interface and do not change game play in any way.

It still isn't foolproof, as I could create an interface which would
give me full knowledge of the game internals (e.g. fully ID every
item, know location of everything on the level, inventory and stats of
all monsters, etc.) and still have save-chains that pass validation.

Other tempting solutions (signed binaries, "trusted computing" and all
that discredited nonsense) simply don't work, even if you are willing
to exclude people who want to compile their own, and even if you are
willing to require the specialized hardware needed.

I can't think of any other methods that would let the general public
swap saves and bones with complete trust, in the face of attackers
determined to subvert the system.

OTOH, the current Hearse model (treat people like adults, and manually
exclude those few who insist on being jackasses) seems to work reasonably
well.

> In an attempt to avoid the long struggle we recently observed in another
> thread I'd like to point out that there are _more than two_ possibilities
> between cryptographically/magically safe and a simply modifyable XML file.

Either your threat model requires that you trust the user, or it does not.

"Have something that appears secure but really isn't" is not a third
option. "Guard against technically unsophisticated attackers, and trust
that smart people know better than to do that" (aka the "inews model")
is not a third option.

Obfuscated XML has costs over and above plaintext (including the cost
of misleading people into thinking it deters editing where it plainly
does not) and no corresponding benefits.

> The current binary data format is (quite) as obscure and as easy/hard
> to be manipulated as an PER encoding would be (which I had used, _if_ I
> would want to achieve portability).[*]

If I wanted to create hokey saves or bones (either in the current game
or in some hypothetical future release with obfuscated file formats of
arbitrarily high complexity), I wouldn't bother messing about with
editing files at all. Instead, I'd just hack the source to, for example,
make /oWishing as common as rocks, and let the game itself write those
files for me.

